## Is IBS a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Severe IBS that has a big effect on daily life can be covered.

## Reasonable adjustments

- a desk **close to a toilet**
- permission to take **toilet breaks** when you need to
- flexible hours, or working from home, during flare-ups
- adjusted travel or meeting arrangements

## Benefits

Most people with IBS will not qualify for PIP. It may help if severe symptoms affect activities on more than half of days, such as:

- **managing toilet needs**, if you need help with incontinence or cleaning up
- **planning and following journeys**, if fear of accidents causes overwhelming distress that stops you going out

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.
